title: 'Stats metadata: the view facts, =(view ...)='
pid: fae0f737-3c2b-4732-a93b-8edb5846515d
body: |2-
  The derived facts of a headline's position stand in one form,
  =(view FACT...)=, after its declared parts and before its =(diff
  ...)=. The server computes them at every rendering (the stats pass,
  =server/refresh/viewnodestats.rs=); nobody reads them back:
  the parser skips =(view ...)= whole, so editing them changes
  nothing. A headline with none carries no =(view ...)=, and =(view)=
  is never written. On a node, in the order written:

  - =omittedBody= — on a write-protected node whose graphnode has a
    body, one the rendering hides. Herald "B", hugging the ☮.
  - =(rels ...)=, =(props ...)=, =(birth ...)= — the relationship
    facts, below.
  - =noWarrant= — the graph does not relate the node to its viewparent
    as its position implies: its parent does not contain it, the
    folder's relation does not hold for it, or, on a chain node, it
    does not contain the node above. A root is always warranted.
    Herald "⊥". Independent of =noClaim=.
  - =cycle= — this position repeats an ancestor's ID. Herald "⟳".
  - =(attribution NAME)= or =(attribution NAME unverified)= — the node
    is a fork with an attribution (=docs/forks.org=, "Attribution").
    NAME is the configured skgrepo whose git remote has the recorded
    remote hash, or, where none was recorded, the configured skgrepo of
    the recorded name, which has no remote either; else the recorded
    name, with =unverified=. It shows nothing itself: the client folds
    it into the home skgrepo herald, "⌂:<HOME/NAME>", "?" after NAME
    when unverified.
  - =repoBoundary= — the node sits at a skgrepo boundary: a root, a
    node whose home skgrepo differs from its nearest occurrence
    ancestor's, or whose attribution differs from that ancestor's
    (one of the two attributed, or both, naming different skgrepos).
    Written only when the config has more than one skgrepo, or an
    attribution is on either side. Herald "⌂".

  On an alias, an ID or a flag member, =(view ...)= holds at most one
  atom: =noWarrant= when the node above its folder lacks it, or
  =ghostWarrant= when HEAD alone warrants it (in the diff view, one
  the worktree lost since HEAD, which also carries =(diff (rel
  removed))=).

  =server/herald_tokens.rs= assembles graph counts and occurrence facts
  into the three semantic forms. Clients render them; the wire contains
  no assembled glyphs, omitted numerals or colors. For example:

  #+begin_example
  (view
    (rels
      (contains (in 2 (ancestors 1))
                (out 8 (ancestors 2) (unintegrated 3 (ancestors 2))))
      (linksTo (in 5 (ancestors 1 2)
                     (substantive 2 (ancestors 1)))
               (out 3 (ancestors 2)))
      (forks (out 1 (ancestors 1))))
    (props (aliases 2) (extraIds 1) (flags 3))
    (birth (contains in 1)))
  #+end_example

  Each relation side in =(rels ...)= is =(in COUNT (ancestors GEN...))=
  or =(out COUNT (ancestors GEN...))=. Empty sides are generally
  omitted, and =(rels ...)= is absent when no relation has anything to
  say; =COUNT= is the complete distinct visible node count even when
  ancestor flags let the client omit its numeral. =GEN= is an ancestor
  generation: 1 is the viewparent, 2 the grandparent, counting
  non-vognode levels. Relation keys are =contains=, =linksTo=,
  =forks=, and =hides=. The =linksTo=
  incoming side adds =(substantive COUNT (ancestors GEN...))= for the
  subset whose skgrepo has a nonempty body, visible direct content, or
  more than one distinct visible resolved target. Its outgoing side
  counts distinct visible resolved targets. An applicable
  member-as-such =contains= outgoing side (a forkee or forker as such)
  adds =(unintegrated COUNT (ancestors GEN...))=, including when both
  counts are zero; absence of that subform means the role does not
  apply. The numerator counts members not already directly contained
  or hidden by the note whose forks folder holds the occurrence, nor
  contained by another note the user owns: what the member shows. Subset counts cannot exceed their side's
  total. The ancestors counted are the viewparent, or, for a folder
  member, the vognodes of the folder's required ancestry. A child
  claiming nothing under a member as such -- content the note hides,
  which the refresh keeps there, unwarranted, while it holds a subtree
  or the user keeps it -- also counts the note, three generations up,
  on its =hides= incoming side, as a =hiddenInForkPartnerFolder=
  member does.

  =(props (aliases K) (extraIds K) (flags K))= holds the property
  counts, each present only when nonzero; =(props ...)= is absent when
  all are zero.

  =(birth (RELNAME SIDE [GEN])...)= identifies the relation(s) behind
  this occurrence, read from where it stands: for each, the side of
  this node it is on (=in= or =out=) and the ancestor generation it
  accounts for. =GEN= is omitted when no ancestor accounts for it, as
  for a node shown in place of a node it forks, which carries
  =(forks out)=. An occurrence that does not stand as a member of its
  viewparent carries none: where the viewparent's claims are read, one
  claiming nothing; in a write-protected folder, one the folder's
  relation does not warrant; under a write-protected occurrence, every
  child ('stands_as_member' in =server/refresh/viewnodestats.rs=). A
  member of a forks folder stands in each role of 'forks' it plays
  ('forks_folder_member_roles'): as a forkee where it claims (where the
  folder reads no claims, where the graph says the folder's viewparent
  forks it), and as a forker where the graph's warrant and a visible
  record say it forks the viewparent, whatever it claims. A mutual
  stands in both, and carries both facts; a detached forkee and an
  indirect member (a mirror of the viewparent joined to it only
  through others) in neither, and carry none.

  | Birth                            | Facts                                                        |
  |----------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------|
  | ordinary content                 | =(contains in 1)=                                            |
  | forks-folder forkee (claiming)   | =(forks in 2)=                                               |
  | forks-folder forker              | =(forks out 2)=                                              |
  | forks-folder mutual              | =(forks in 2) (forks out 2)=                                 |
  | forks-folder indirect member     | none                                                         |
  | hidden-folder member             | =(hides in 2)=                                |
  | hider-folder member              | =(hides out 2)=                               |
  | container-folder member          | =(contains out 2)=                            |
  | mentioner-folder member          | =(linksTo out 2)=                             |
  | integrated-elsewhere member      | =(contains in 2)=                             |
  | hidden-in-fork-partner member    | =(hides in 4) (contains in 2)=                |
  | hidden-by-fork-partner member    | =(hides in 2) (contains in 4)=                |
  | hidden-outside member            | =(hides in 3)=                                |
  | mirror shown (inPlaceOf)     | =(forks out)= or =(forks in)=                                |
  A member of an acquisition folder carries none: a nodeMerge is no
  relation. In a forks folder the relationship heralds tell the sides
  apart: a forkee shows =bK= (its viewparent's viewparent, 'b', forks
  it), a forker =Kb=, a mutual =bKb=, an indirect member neither 'b'
  flag ('docs/heralds.org').
  See [[id:ef1f3f60-2e51-4584-8e5d-7dd6f6214cd2][heralds.org]] for the rendered notation and styles.

  The graph counts fold into these forms (=forks= / =hides=
  become the =K= / =H= tokens; =aliasing= / =extraIDs= /
  true flags become the =Ak= / =Ik= / =Fk= property tokens), and the
  per-relation view-position flags are the ancestor-lettered tokens.

  Two facts of a headline's relationship to its viewparent are not view
  facts. The skgrepo recording it, when its privacy was deliberately
  raised above the relationship's default, is the =(relRepo NAME)= of
  the headline's =(skgRepos ...)= (the =contains= relationship to a
  content child, or the folder's relation for a partner folder member;
  for a member of a forks folder, its viewparent's record of the fork
  where it claims, else its own record of forking the viewparent, on
  which a relRepo request is refused);
  ugly herald "~NAME", shown immediately before the ⌂. A relationship
  recorded in a foreign skgrepo never carries it: a foreign skgrepo has
  no privacy rank, and only its own node records the relationship. It is recomputed
  at every rendering, and the save never reads it as a request, which
  is =(editRequest (relRepo NAME))=. And =(inPlaceOf N)=, crucial
  =ĥ= immediately after the =K= token, is a load-bearing declared part,
  documented in "Mirror substitution and the inPlaceOf part"
  below.
